## 3. The slop this session produced — contained here, must not be merged

Branch `claude/overnight-2026-11-26` (8 commits: `magloop`+`sectorground`, `sectorcharge`,
`transport`, `twovertex`, `edgehol`, `pairenergy`, `pathtransport`, `chargeclass`).

**What it claims.** That the electric energy on the spin network is a class function of the multiset
of vertex charges — separation-independent for every configuration — so "the force between charges is
absent from the electric term; it lives entirely in the kinetic hop cost." (No confinement from the
electric term.)

**Why it is wrong — from the module's own lines.** `chem/pairenergy.hypercubical` models the electric
energy as

```
vertex-cost m = bal-sq m                    -- least Σ_e n_e² at a vertex of |charge| m
total-cost ns = Σ_v vertex-cost(n_v)        -- sum over vertices, independent terms
pair-path k   = [1, 0×k, 1]                 -- a ±1 pair with k neutral vertices between
pair-cost-k   : total-cost(pair-path k) = 2 -- "same at any separation"   (proof λ i → true)
```

Each intermediate vertex is allowed to relax to its own in-sector minimum, and a neutral vertex
contributes `bal-sq(0) = 0`. That is exactly dropping the Faraday flux string. Gauss's law on the
graph, `div n = δ₊ − δ₋` (proved in `chem/cover`), forces nonzero flux edge-labels on every edge of
the path separating the two charges — the connecting vertices cannot all be neutral while a unit of
flux threads across the cut. `chem/fluxsplit` (on `main`) proves the resulting least energy is the
shorter arc, growing as `min(d, N−d)` with separation: a confining string. The `pair-cost-k = 2`
lemmas are arithmetically valid (`λ i → true` typechecks only because `nat-eq (total-cost …) 2`
reduces to `true`), but they are lemmas about a model that omits the string, and the headline
conclusion directly contradicts `chem/fluxsplit`, which is already on `main`.

`sectorcharge`/`sectorground` additionally re-derive, at lower rigor, the on-vertex result that
`chem/sectors` already proves on `main` (electric energy at a four-valent vertex of charge `N` depends
on `N` alone, the 625 sectors gathering to `(q,0,0,0)`). So the branch is a lower-rigor duplicate of
`chem/sectors` plus a false generalization. It meets none of the bar and is not merged.

